{#-
  The imperial nails this repository builds, as the standards state them.

  Every number below is in inches, the unit the source tables are written in;
  'imperial.yaml' converts them to millimetres (INCH = 25.4) on the way to
  the script, which works in millimetres like everything else in PartCAD.

  Where they come from:

  * ASTM F1667 is the specification these nails are bought to. Its Table 1
    gives every style an identifier and a table of its own - those are the
    'f1667' and 'table' fields - and section 8 says how a nail is measured:
    from under the head where the head is flat, overall otherwise.

  * The dimensions are those of US Federal Specification FF-N-105B, which
    ASTM F1667 replaced and which is public: the 'source' field names the
    paragraph each family is copied from. ASTM F1667's own tables are not
    reproduced here. Where the two can be compared they agree - F1667's
    example part number NLCL-09 is "a cooler nail with a length of 2-7/8,
    a shank diameter of 0.120, and a head diameter of 0.297", which is row 9
    of the cooler table below - and so does MiTek's published table for the
    common and sinker sizes it stocks. That is not the same as having
    checked every row against F1667, and a row that disagrees with a copy of
    F1667 should be corrected to it.

  * The connector nails are not an F1667 style. They are common-nail wire
    cut short for the holes in joist hangers and other metal connectors, and
    their sizes are from MiTek's nail table. That table states no head, so
    theirs is the head of the common nail of the same pennyweight, and its
    counts per pound are for coated nails, so none is given for them here.

  Each row is:

      [name, L, D, H, approximate count per pound]

  L is the length as the family's 'measured' says, D the shank diameter
  before any deformation (F1667 8.3), H the head diameter. The count per
  pound is the source's, for bright smooth nails; nothing builds from it,
  but it is what a nail's volume times the density of steel has to come
  back to, and that is how this table was checked against 'nail.py'. Where
  a nail is mostly shank the two agree to within about 10 %. Where it is mostly
  head - roofing and smooth gypsum-board nails, the smallest sinkers - the
  model is up to a third heavier, because no table states how thick a head
  is and 'nail.py' has to assume one. Three of the source's own counts are
  low for its own dimensions whatever the head: the 2d and 3d cooler and
  the 60d common.

  'shank', 'point' and 'coating' are each family's default, as its source
  describes it. Every one of them can be asked for differently: ASTM F1667
  makes deformed shanks and the coatings a matter of what was ordered.
-#}

